# rules: java-credential-is-a-literal
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-798.java
# from: CWE-798 (Use of Hard-coded Credentials), CWE-259
#
# A password written into a `getConnection` call is in version control, in the
# build artifact, and readable out of the jar with `strings`. It cannot be
# rotated without a release, everyone who has ever cloned the repository still
# knows it, and it is usually the same password on staging as on production,
# because writing it down is what made that convenient.
#
# The position is what makes this checkable without guessing. `getConnection`
# takes the password third and nothing else; `setPassword` and
# `setProperty("password", ...)` say what they are. So the rule asks about an
# argument in a credential position and does not go looking for names that
# sound like secrets, which is the version of this rule that reports every
# constant in a program.
#
# Two literals are excluded and both exclusions matter more than they look:
#
#   - the empty string. A local H2 or SQLite database with no password is the
#     usual reason for `getConnection(url, "sa", "")`, and it is not a secret
#     that leaked.
#   - `${...}` and `#{...}`. That is a Spring or Ant placeholder naming a
#     value that lives somewhere else, which is the fix. Reporting it would
#     train people to skip this rule on exactly the codebases that got it
#     right.
#   - the literal that is the *name* of the thing rather than a value of it:
#     `setPassword("password")`, `("pwd2")`, `("pass")`, `("foo")`. Nobody's
#     production password is the string `password`; these are what a test
#     writes when it needs an argument. Spring's own suite has twenty-eight of
#     them and twenty-three are the single word `password`, which is the count
#     that made this exclusion necessary rather than tidy.
#
# `changeit` is deliberately *not* excluded. It is the default password of
# every Java keystore, and finding it left in is the point.
#
# A constant field - `private static final String PW = "..."` - is not
# reported, because a field declaration is not a shape a pattern can name in
# this engine. java-key-from-a-literal says more about that gap.

["DriverManager.getConnection($_, $_, $PW)",
 "$DS.setPassword($PW)", "$DS.setCredentials($PW)",
 "new UsernamePasswordCredentials($_, $PW)",
 "new PasswordAuthentication($_, $PW)",
 "new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken($_, $PW)",
 "new BasicAWSCredentials($_, $PW)"] as $positional
| ["$P.setProperty($K, $PW)", "$P.put($K, $PW)"] as $keyed
| "^\"[^\"]" as $literal
| "^\"\\$\\{|^\"#\\{|^\"%\\(|^\"<" as $placeholder
| ( "(?i)^\"(pass(word)?|passwd|pwd|pw|secret|credential|token|key|"
    + "user(name)?|admin|root|foo|bar|baz|qux|test|example|sample|dummy|"
    + "placeholder|value|string|none|null|todo|x+|\\*+|\\.+)[0-9]?\"$" ) as $theWordItself
| "(?i)^\"(pass|password|passwd|pwd|secret|credential|api[._-]?key|auth[._-]?token)\"$" as $credentialKey
| scan_ast("*.java"; $positional + $keyed) as $all
| ( ($all | of($positional))
    + ($all | of($keyed) | where_capture("K"; $credentialKey)) )
| where_capture("PW"; $literal)
| where_capture_not("PW"; $placeholder)
| where_capture_not("PW"; $theWordItself)
| focus("PW")
| finding("java-credential-is-a-literal";
    "this password is written into the program, so it is in version control "
    + "and in the jar, and changing it means shipping a release")
| report
